  "established_findings": [
    "One camera header jumped backward by 534.425ms while its hardware timestamp and metadata frame_number advanced normally; host realtime advanced normally. This is camera global-time mapping instability, not a 534ms host clock rollback or missing physical exposure.",
    "Original input handling clears all source histories on any per-source backward timestamp. This camera timestamp correction produced a roughly 0.958s recording wait, reported as ROS time rollback.",
    "Native single, dual and triple camera 120s windows were clean at the current wiring; two full 60s ROS/recording windows showed right RGB source gaps before RCL publication. No entered-publish delivery losses in these two windows and no writer queue exhaustion.",
    "Earlier right USB resets remain separate documented evidence; current normal native windows do not establish a permanent hardware fix or identify camera/cable/port as root cause."
  ],
  "next_steps": [
    "Handle camera timestamp startup convergence and source-specific regressions; retain truthful timestamps and explicit discontinuity boundaries rather than fabricating frames.",
    "Distinguish original camera exposure gaps from 30-to-20Hz nearest-image selection jumps in diagnostics.",
    "For residual UVC sequence gaps/older resets, a physically verified wrist camera-plus-cable cross-port comparison remains necessary to isolate port versus assembly."
